#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} {:answer/id #uuid "14581a73-ac53-505c-8447-0394a875fcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247608} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb5a940-7e41-5de0-9f76-33adbc3d81da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6d5bb5-c38f-58f9-bdcf-296b1625b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249109} {:answer/id #uuid "de549bfd-d213-57c1-ad88-fadc596d833b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a248b79-7e8b-5ad8-b2e8-847ac2030783" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Square Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55f584c-0261-59b4-bb28-5df04c5e5bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051333} {:answer/id #uuid "df503ec5-393a-5e2d-b982-c56e35aaa181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051334} {:answer/id #uuid "03de6fb6-f81c-5d37-a647-7b5873b78089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051335} {:answer/id #uuid "423881fd-5f5f-52b5-95c3-bdb632772bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051336} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb4b1ee-1844-5475-bfe1-7abebf91b3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051337}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8876c980-9b7f-5aa5-b5a7-7c5722252f32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e60215-8077-5f8c-8c55-359a0a908320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386738} {:answer/id #uuid "29d738c0-81db-5e6c-91c9-db679852e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386739} {:answer/id #uuid "20fa7a5b-5a7b-5d2e-91fe-afbafad3502d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386740} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d7ca1e-94f6-54ec-80f6-9ab401480cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386741} {:answer/id #uuid "8751a674-6cb2-575b-98a1-e1863e33ff8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d7ca1e-94f6-54ec-80f6-9ab401480cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce63358-0fcc-55be-a22f-c3c4afd97cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06528850-f3e1-5360-9392-bba2a3bf7deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-11440" :question/problem "Calculate $A+B$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4 & 5+1 \\\\ 3+(-2) & 1+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f66da87-fd41-50fb-b1c2-717ae0591859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247850} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffb2392-7201-5c3f-88bc-392e23cd505a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247855} {:answer/id #uuid "32b16bed-d3ba-546b-bd37-eb68ce75789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247885} {:answer/id #uuid "4204141e-6e99-5199-acf5-81445a1bf4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248012} {:answer/id #uuid "659e5468-e099-55eb-a4ef-9a48327cdd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f66da87-fd41-50fb-b1c2-717ae0591859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247850} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9dadf63-5d8c-56c1-8844-878ea2a7844a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ed9bfbd-adb2-54e5-bf5d-6caa34443565" :question/math-academy-id "q-115031" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1 & \\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2x+1} & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{2x+1})}^{3} & \\le {3}^{3} \\\\ 2x+1 & \\le 27 \\\\ 2x & \\le 26 \\\\ x & \\le 13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486cff62-01d3-5049-9232-7e6b8711ae02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248073} {:answer/id #uuid "735df79d-ddb4-5640-bc31-d30cdbd2b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248433} {:answer/id #uuid "a517b323-5082-5ab0-976c-3adc8f5377c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248898} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0187fb-9d9a-5be9-8904-397388709c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249085} {:answer/id #uuid "beede94a-cbfc-5628-8c7c-ee54767e0e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735df79d-ddb4-5640-bc31-d30cdbd2b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248433} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4f1ea4-5984-59d1-8aad-4caa825a8331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76685609-49ea-56b3-87fb-774aa7271a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115101" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-22}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-22 & =5(x-4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-22 & =5(4-4)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de557d1d-5f27-50e3-bcba-22b21f83267f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153668da-d780-523a-8e26-48e058715b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247615} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9dc02d-1da3-5526-af85-e13cc50a7c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247968} {:answer/id #uuid "750c26fb-43a5-5a41-8361-58c46e0b0804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d902f3-35d6-5d0d-ac8b-52610f9c819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248870} {:answer/id #uuid "a8569992-7633-5a8b-9630-e609ef25ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "750c26fb-43a5-5a41-8361-58c46e0b0804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} :answer-field/id #uuid "106279dc-5bad-5b89-83c9-6a40e4f9b6f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "522f5175-a019-5a90-95ed-58cc045a0980" :question/math-academy-id "q-115124" :question/problem "![](images/76/764f25a4b18f3f28110c0ce3345402bf183d61cac350832663e221b0de9852a5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bdd76d-fa8d-58df-b8b1-e545dd5c28a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247444} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8022bf-d528-51f6-a8fe-a1a2f2edfe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248288} {:answer/id #uuid "72f0f4a1-3bd5-58b1-99bb-22f7c3a5d087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248429} {:answer/id #uuid "7b638bf8-7756-522e-9993-bf13fac41704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248508} {:answer/id #uuid "e798bb82-b211-5b83-b9ba-7f365819f211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02bdd76d-fa8d-58df-b8b1-e545dd5c28a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247444} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d28fe8-a9db-5ee3-a3b6-13de7bfcbfad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c7e7d0e-6a38-575b-b57f-c4b68672e279" :question/math-academy-id "q-115205" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2{e}^{x-2}+1<3.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbca64e-b64c-56ce-8eb6-ecc6fa7d24a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247969} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcc2019-801d-5716-bfaf-80f9a09a03a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248526} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2be079-dea0-55fc-b357-78c9dfb9a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248655} {:answer/id #uuid "8eeafaf3-5a99-5006-982b-a9ce701b9d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248693} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ebd06e-cdc5-52a8-b4dd-4e79454aff1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a2be079-dea0-55fc-b357-78c9dfb9a15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248655} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f468e2b-e0a3-5878-a496-8f74d00ba6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a9eca33-ce1c-5c81-8c1b-83778e94422b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115214" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}-6(2)+9 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-6(0)+9 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0-2)+1 \\\\ 9 & =4A-2B+1 \\\\ 0 & =4A-2B-8 \\\\ 0 & =2A-B-4 \\\\ B & =2A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=1$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}-6(1)+9 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+1 \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-3 \\\\ B & =A-3.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A-4 & =A-3 \\\\ 2A-A & =-3+4 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =A-3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bdffb0b-c92f-5db5-9e16-6a3a24c0abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248676} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f077c7-5e76-5947-a22b-b6009f1fd675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.72+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249458} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f9e7c7-c57e-5eff-8f9d-5369d8005008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 7-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249473} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b4cb88-3f73-51d9-8cf2-e22028d822b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249563} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c7af93-9f26-5f0f-a636-8636e12184c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9c7af93-9f26-5f0f-a636-8636e12184c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249600} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a6f05f-9154-583e-9c93-e25bd8295455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4c4542-894b-5890-9a4c-cece675d50b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115230" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2\\le -4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2 & \\le -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -6 \\\\ (0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.72)^{3x-7}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.72)^{3x-7}\\le -12$ has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e749b95-3859-5caf-bfd8-c834793e8ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247694} {:answer/id #uuid "40bebdef-ae8a-5d9e-ac28-6228e738db2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247995} {:answer/id #uuid "49f23bbb-2f76-5966-bbae-3c9d22acc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248082} {:answer/id #uuid "b99ff983-b6ef-5e58-8aa0-af927de0b3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249083} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c71b-a6be-59ae-b32b-dde21ce862fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49f23bbb-2f76-5966-bbae-3c9d22acc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248082} :answer-field/id #uuid "7730106b-6087-572a-9b32-b26564608b47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c08f0a1-4fee-59ab-b31f-f6a9816dc7a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115250" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${5}^{x-1}<{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b832b4-be06-5538-a94b-59818cb45058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247571} {:answer/id #uuid "14b511b3-c177-5a70-857b-b7eb87042c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247610} {:answer/id #uuid "9502705b-7b3a-50f1-ba5b-ab33c1d821fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x>7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248748} {:answer/id #uuid "aa53455f-6c45-505a-ba7b-d31e4ed8c1fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-7$ or $x>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248940} {:answer/id #uuid "c91c552a-e3ed-5137-931f-0e712dfaf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7<x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91c552a-e3ed-5137-931f-0e712dfaf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7<x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249214} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1df4b5a-1a4a-56c9-9f2c-02ac2d39edd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e9f024f-9fc0-58af-9612-031b9867fc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-115287" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb57d377a05a6e169c32f2442e02c3926391d62bff7cb24cf3201fb0289b59a4.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $7-6x-{x}^{2}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n7-6x-{x}^{2}>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=7-6x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e12975187d46b6dc408e21234309e33654a24503db65ed075e32990de8ed654.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-7<x<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8d6fa-6312-5332-b890-b96f8ba86d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247690} {:answer/id #uuid "2f504533-dba5-5a4f-8e8e-d571f324bc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247849} {:answer/id #uuid "5337ac8c-87dc-5ed1-9732-75915073f600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248174} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7cb839-e5f3-5581-9fe8-bb0945fd74b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248373} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7020d4-fe3f-50f8-bd90-be3c6e4d9e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7020d4-fe3f-50f8-bd90-be3c6e4d9e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248543} :answer-field/id #uuid "54c42180-98c9-5c11-b93b-3fa148651bca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dd86485-ed9c-5475-8932-90607bb9958e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115308" :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-2$ and $k=3,$ so the center is located at $(-2,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53855cff-bcca-509b-8df4-37d79e809e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399873} {:answer/id #uuid "17302a0c-db71-5e47-9f9c-7d55635451b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399874} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399875} {:answer/id #uuid "f17c689d-e9fa-5fd9-b2ff-5ea750ce8ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399876} {:answer/id #uuid "d786fe68-0f02-5397-bafb-ab8b7df3332f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399875} :answer-field/id #uuid "632f4b79-0b5d-599f-9a9d-155f44ffca58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6821b43a-88ac-5b4b-9151-2f35f973a4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115313" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-5$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(-5,2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=25$ and ${b}^{2}=6.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=6,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=2\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100994 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e9df1ae-8176-555b-987d-94773c27d435" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane Using the Distance Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2172b8f7-3593-5571-b2f3-7d7267020abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247720} {:answer/id #uuid "39cf299f-3302-568e-80c6-f68f90479350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247934} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb4096d-f189-5fca-82c4-8803e1e12ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248291} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0f207e-3753-5536-ba31-32a146709a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248381} {:answer/id #uuid "dca5ff4b-c86a-5b8a-b547-f8671207d2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c0f207e-3753-5536-ba31-32a146709a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248381} :answer-field/id #uuid "cffa1467-5032-5c7c-8355-c1c0efa2e0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fa395af-9563-5dd6-b788-298fc3edabb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115360" :question/problem "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-3,-4)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Find the radius of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3-0)^{2}+(-4-(-4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100995 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52384502-66c5-5089-b38f-d46b94877430" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fa6bba-8366-50e3-bd6e-0487665ca2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399887} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba675a-9578-5d7e-86a6-668cee3df4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399888} {:answer/id #uuid "73d0aee8-773e-58fb-89a9-36098d20a922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399889} {:answer/id #uuid "d14d98e0-0f4f-5aa7-b716-c80926e97a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399890} {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a898ac5-9ac8-57ea-9a3a-e0a38e6b53ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02e3599-0d24-591c-985f-c45e9f652ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115364" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3af3101fd7a103e8382755177ca1ebab9bc1cdf48aebd34af8ac7cc92991387c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1,4)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{6}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,3)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-0)}^{2}+{(2-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826552d3-c4e0-538c-9196-4afb18110140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248579} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf08c39-e90f-5ba4-9229-d2ffff374c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-13)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248811} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb2522e-32d2-56e6-ac37-54a4312dd677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (13,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248847} {:answer/id #uuid "af81a493-1449-5b36-ab97-412b7aebe26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-13)\\cup (13,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248998} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1caebc-93ec-52f4-b0af-a5dbfeb7b5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1caebc-93ec-52f4-b0af-a5dbfeb7b5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-13,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249251} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa7acea-63e1-5f4b-ad99-c211d52b1202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2bb17ed-6922-578d-aad8-d1768d7a7b6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115426" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+13x<0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+13x & <0 \\\\ x(x+13) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+13)=0.$ The roots are $x=-13$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-13),$ $(-13,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-13)$ | $(-13,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+13$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+13)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+13)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+13)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-13,0).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+13x<0$ for all $x\\in (-13,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80063ee4-5a2e-54d5-a7c0-7f53301ee070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248547} {:answer/id #uuid "942c6f19-e871-5efb-bc84-cc274cf8d7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248741} {:answer/id #uuid "aa477baf-669c-5ca1-9535-44663ae87ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=16y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248939} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8ef81-1c40-56e6-b62a-55e8bb45c5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249148} {:answer/id #uuid "f0990477-9c09-5cd5-b650-146fa848ec1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8ef81-1c40-56e6-b62a-55e8bb45c5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249148} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc0ca73-8e47-548c-a716-86b46758af6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88252d6a-eb99-5de5-8ab4-de1f72848e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-115432" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(1)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} {:answer/id #uuid "6d01c7a7-ed12-581a-857f-30f616b9ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248388} {:answer/id #uuid "711453e3-73f9-5722-820f-ac643ba5fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248413} {:answer/id #uuid "8d999a07-30ce-573b-8c2d-fa8220991104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248686} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aed572-9d95-5182-8bd1-9ea1faa87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} :answer-field/id #uuid "242c488e-b1d2-5f9b-924c-0c83035c9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af2d74a7-36e3-5f6f-95f7-10b5d7e5decf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115451" :question/problem "![](images/97/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448f46ef-d3ea-5129-b534-69738ce2bf22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248036} {:answer/id #uuid "61d14d47-1b22-5269-bf65-c45079b61a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248307} {:answer/id #uuid "96e2c46f-fcee-5729-87c3-3a89d53c7346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248769} {:answer/id #uuid "c2981009-de70-58f9-bf1b-475e3952da5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249154} {:answer/id #uuid "d1808623-7ad0-5bda-a910-99c16db5520e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d14d47-1b22-5269-bf65-c45079b61a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248307} :answer-field/id #uuid "7857cb4e-c7be-5b90-8d5b-a7a24b7b056d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3ec34c5-8643-5bc8-b96a-2dfe405443a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115502" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6b336b55e48a68dcad83efc4a8acd7659225b8f8e29faf8ea176c2cead29901.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-4-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-1) \\\\ 36 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-3)(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a986a9-dc99-5796-9957-e44b0b8d9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247416} {:answer/id #uuid "3af46962-42bf-52d0-9044-0ca1c67c1f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247946} {:answer/id #uuid "40f40afb-bd2f-53ad-bfee-4cedf94cb572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248000} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b7d8e-f5b6-5077-a852-7535675aef4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a77272b0-ce4e-560d-9231-c7789014a60c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c7e4a7-0bdd-5125-954e-1f3a2bdbf235" :question/math-academy-id "q-115513" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b693811-a16b-53ed-9611-68583bdf136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247672} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b941c-69b2-577b-a723-cb390aecaad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247825} {:answer/id #uuid "52a8d6c1-c802-52b6-9422-b177b1c39eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248165} {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0fcf86-6e40-5292-948f-778a8f8317e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb56519-add1-5a22-b89f-f23b73f9e1db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b7651b-eacd-5715-8331-dab57a1d4302" :question/math-academy-id "q-115526" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d037599-07ed-5ba7-83e9-f8c1efa482f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247821} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae4547-e21a-58af-ba30-0bdc4c6faf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247874} {:answer/id #uuid "88431084-d59b-514f-a06a-1a663f1974b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248641} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} {:answer/id #uuid "f67649d3-0d90-50b6-8568-82074b23672e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f9bf5f-e290-5c3d-ab0c-661753693c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e116ffff-24eb-5f64-952b-b5d8dd36f71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115530" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45166553-bf16-5fa9-8f50-1ec180cc0491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{16}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248043} {:answer/id #uuid "869aa896-1386-5aa1-acd0-77b1926c3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248625} {:answer/id #uuid "b1716195-2d70-5b16-8e29-18c8d7c74eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249013} {:answer/id #uuid "cad70ee1-b661-5e1b-8e0a-c2d157b64a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249231} {:answer/id #uuid "d07d22e0-e6d0-514f-92c9-e09c9996024c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1716195-2d70-5b16-8e29-18c8d7c74eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249013} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c6191f-54f1-5592-9add-c8743f8cc4c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07da239d-b796-56b0-87bd-44779be7e860" :question/math-academy-id "q-115634" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-4)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} {:answer/id #uuid "839ff22d-c37d-5d91-9333-174668c3261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27305a-d65b-5604-aaff-169ce339bb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249088} {:answer/id #uuid "c6443236-97c8-5e59-91fe-9a22a7cec2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249186} {:answer/id #uuid "dc878183-0dd0-5e61-ae12-26bab8ba8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{12}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f52642-018d-53e4-af4e-c4f058d871f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff1310e-72c2-5e26-9574-a4434ee14fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115642" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096705 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1250a4f0-3718-52c9-9904-980960e90f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247590} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef0ec03-c73b-5bdb-afad-8cb320620573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247845} {:answer/id #uuid "9501f1cc-1079-55a6-ae61-087d9b006e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248747} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f2ce1-958b-58a2-8358-7d53986a1a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249460} {:answer/id #uuid "f753f4ac-c307-5fb5-a501-f7cc4a07aeb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72f2ce1-958b-58a2-8358-7d53986a1a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249460} :answer-field/id #uuid "07d422bf-e80a-5532-a92f-ce3f3b453131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4025b5ab-d584-549e-8e48-80f0cd57c2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-1),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(2-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(1)(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c7a77cf-163a-542a-b4a3-4274344b9cf8" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying 3x3 Square Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a311bb5-3182-5198-a152-bc200f70be62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248372} {:answer/id #uuid "93e14b07-a873-5463-ab6a-2c7bc9069278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248739} {:answer/id #uuid "a450ea4a-394f-5c90-bf03-56556c7878ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248890} {:answer/id #uuid "af58b380-5f76-5bbb-a820-5f4b83135e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248996} {:answer/id #uuid "f0734682-f5a2-5e21-a23e-5f8cb703cff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a450ea4a-394f-5c90-bf03-56556c7878ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248890} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9354821-08c3-542c-978e-86f64daf4fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c07fc8a-7bd0-54d4-b86d-f5ce5f6701b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-11568" :question/problem "Find $ST$,  if  $S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+0+8 & 2+0-4 & 3+0+4 \\\\ 0-5+4 & 0+5-2 & 0-0+2 \\\\ 6+0+4 & 4-0-2 & 6+0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} {:answer/id #uuid "284a2d2d-aec2-56b2-a83a-447fe88b345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247782} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2d177-bb80-5fbb-b4fb-239d54aa4023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a88c5c8-97bc-5272-bbaa-7a0f74513dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248802} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb1cf3-0a3b-5e0b-81f5-f88d8973a54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d68dd-7d98-50ea-b0e6-bb084af9ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ff28b3-a4ef-5634-a723-9c7e9fe2ab39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247714} {:answer/id #uuid "98d4e748-2168-5cb4-bb3d-c851125d1b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248788} {:answer/id #uuid "abcbd97a-5003-5b7a-8d19-a85482f993a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248952} {:answer/id #uuid "cecdc329-36c7-5062-90f6-c9e193b82b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249256} {:answer/id #uuid "e601ffa8-cd81-5d33-b1c2-e93f104c920b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cecdc329-36c7-5062-90f6-c9e193b82b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249256} :answer-field/id #uuid "858767bb-4413-5728-bc93-27047974679c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47ce069d-e0d7-5b9d-a02a-3975e0c397e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115881" :question/problem "![](images/72/725cbaa4ce797e0436d1a53d4b1a9eedad5a15282fadfa8cb8f3381135e92d87.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80f4e7505c204572ecbc3c2e1c207fa9f321bc9925f5e2e947db7feee45824f9.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(6,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e302810-8b14-5c8c-8bbb-245386a66b7c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106bf08a-6288-5161-b589-b3a6bfd5d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247568} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecf6f9d-a2f5-586a-b320-3685a62fe9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248538} {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} {:answer/id #uuid "e75adbb9-dff5-55d0-b063-3054aec88c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249463} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0d9b69-2f3f-545f-8357-505ac2a681cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} :answer-field/id #uuid "791f4c31-853e-56a1-9a6c-8d9759f6db39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115911" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316d7132-e4d2-552b-8229-76cf4a760b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247871} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9febdf-840d-5e4a-9454-270334fac866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248674} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19e8fc-2e30-562b-bb53-409259921842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249096} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb9b84b-92e1-5992-bbf6-9dbd59a7b2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249366} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c934-c5fd-5811-a685-9aa2936725ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c934-c5fd-5811-a685-9aa2936725ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249432} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9b725b-1554-5fc4-b7bb-be67fcd944ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35bb8fc9-b273-5104-bbf5-373700e22296" :question/math-academy-id "q-116100" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-1$ and $k=5.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d15b2a-d840-5012-b070-e3462c9da9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(36,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247594} {:answer/id #uuid "247c9c2b-5862-5eab-b7d6-5bc4e81f7699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247743} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c70e60-f026-55b3-9d72-4a1fbcbe300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248861} {:answer/id #uuid "dd02f9e6-3fef-5510-9fef-c8504811fe08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249383} {:answer/id #uuid "e539cb36-7d2c-587b-a892-73405cb109ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e539cb36-7d2c-587b-a892-73405cb109ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249442} :answer-field/id #uuid "667ba501-b342-5000-bcdf-f4159e0d68ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a54ae2-f488-56eb-9b24-9f9d28627e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-116103" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-6))^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-6,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237b559-7d33-5cd0-9da8-8d8b205f4742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99e31-a544-5220-80a8-c1892b899c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "cac77d12-ae34-5ddd-8afb-806b1d42068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368912-431d-5c1c-94c8-d1e252061d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05de44ca-2588-5951-97b7-06f8b0b05597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8f012-4b8e-59d2-86f2-6cf9ded76584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247549} {:answer/id #uuid "1d73eb71-f3b0-5802-bd97-3518478f6143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247688} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e6b00-01a7-50ba-8b49-1508e2c1ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248267} {:answer/id #uuid "93a15523-e8ae-509a-8f92-1e5d130afcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248737} {:answer/id #uuid "f70239d3-779d-5762-a4c5-9498427f2ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e6b00-01a7-50ba-8b49-1508e2c1ebb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248267} :answer-field/id #uuid "465002b5-f411-5a4c-a8ea-09bf4dac334e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09ea155c-8ce4-5cf1-a51e-cc7b1072dc21" :question/math-academy-id "q-116116" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x=-122?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-60y-4{x}^{2}+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}-10y]-4[{x}^{2}-2x] & =-122 \\\\ 6[(y-5)^{2}-25]-4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-150-4(x-1)^{2}+4 & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =-122+150-4 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df10f3e-d14c-5f31-9dc9-16f77013f823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247971} {:answer/id #uuid "4117a38e-ba73-54ec-ae01-b02155c3cdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248002} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2d3d9-ea09-55b2-9def-1d2c0b62c87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248616} {:answer/id #uuid "b4058eef-7428-5278-bb14-46fdc13aef8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249035} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a52fc-f6b8-51a2-a64e-ae084b1ad986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df10f3e-d14c-5f31-9dc9-16f77013f823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247971} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce871a56-e8e2-5311-94d2-e7cf98dbc923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f895ed60-2cc3-5f28-b4ed-ecac286a96ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-116200" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+3x-10) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-2).$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-2)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0f80f8-48a0-54be-83dd-0ca6ee45bd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250790} {:answer/id #uuid "35e3a955-88c2-5a1a-ac81-4b5e743b374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251002} {:answer/id #uuid "5612757d-6a5f-5d7b-8231-e56ae23f5735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251259} {:answer/id #uuid "93e2fc3d-4f00-59d6-9429-7bf2268d114a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251824} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf26cc3-65b3-5f84-a9e3-f670b3919722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e2fc3d-4f00-59d6-9429-7bf2268d114a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251824} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f9c632-e83a-5f16-bf5c-d762296eeb77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04531fbf-424a-55fe-857b-3e4c5af620eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-116269" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }\\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi <-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\le (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ 2x+3 & \\le 2-7x \\\\ 9x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203d12f3-8c3a-5557-837d-97614900aecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250827} {:answer/id #uuid "37fa1324-7dc6-58cd-a405-664a3029c979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251017} {:answer/id #uuid "43c9ad1f-5027-564e-8d6f-f65d7b6af604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251120} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5992ac-076a-5473-a6d9-3e4bba83cb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251599} {:answer/id #uuid "b34057e3-6fe3-51a0-8d42-660e355aaf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34057e3-6fe3-51a0-8d42-660e355aaf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252122} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c14b9f0-5ae4-545c-9581-c501daf9503a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297364cd-4444-5a41-ba36-21b226267ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11637" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $CD=DC={I}_{2}$\n- $DE=ED={I}_{2}$\n- $D={C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e5043a-3f1b-5f5b-bcbc-e166efd4ebcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250707} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4b2875-f02c-5afa-b32c-36d4cf303e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251719} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce64a4f-ea12-5a34-9745-a6ee77838e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251907} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e3439e-fc9c-56a7-abef-e1dbb6bcab4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252329} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cbebeb-2715-5dea-b1bf-de479f36bf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4cbebeb-2715-5dea-b1bf-de479f36bf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252697} :answer-field/id #uuid "4480b1db-53d1-58b7-9c5c-51fba18c5921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f270ff5-bc17-53a5-be2e-3042a3d54393" :question/math-academy-id "q-116595" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,7),\\,(7,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e9a537-35f9-5e3b-ab01-9a35c8a25c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-7,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251657} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c2a7c7-94dc-5448-b33b-a91741c663a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251941} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0e19d0-9307-5c69-9e89-ae8e019adbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252344} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e009d-1001-5e69-8ef1-819444c324cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252353} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bc0ca2-d579-5a1c-98a4-d0d580ba1284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bc0ca2-d579-5a1c-98a4-d0d580ba1284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252456} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eea42f5-3c3e-56b9-8e85-3dd91f7a6a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a117df1a-bd81-5da6-88f9-198bb5436e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-116614" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e75012ec-8203-574b-ba07-0a26e4cf4691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given Endpoints of a Diameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58b042e9-ade1-5935-8496-7739089d2bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251280} {:answer/id #uuid "7dabc331-8583-5eea-8639-2e4e3b6c2e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251605} {:answer/id #uuid "b16389d5-034e-5416-a93f-c8ca3c3cb569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} {:answer/id #uuid "cd64e8ae-6767-597d-94ad-605f327334be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252363} {:answer/id #uuid "f51bee25-4a1e-5318-a9c6-0478a7be24bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b16389d5-034e-5416-a93f-c8ca3c3cb569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252105} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d216b22-c302-5a1c-933a-a0dd19c71347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e38a793-c9a3-5cf1-9d57-1f88f87f48f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116927" :question/problem "![](images/34/343897550f0f7b9e4a479e47c9191ababa31070dac79dbf4d54befe869661a48.png)\n\nGiven that the points $X$ and $Y$ with coordinates $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-1)^{2}+(2-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110568 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e370276-dd3a-5964-9ce3-7cf246014e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/628d53eca69ad50cab7557ede3a7f418264203fefd54adac965a7f7f446ece6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386843} {:answer/id #uuid "ffedee4a-7324-59fb-a847-2555b4c482e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386844} {:answer/id #uuid "52d82c0b-587c-5e13-b7a8-b445f168e155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cb152b659ff0b75f70c8c7235357790d81c673df270de6b8dcb44bced33f39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386845} {:answer/id #uuid "69cca405-61ef-5beb-98b6-f0ac8830d33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d75e53dde04a14f588e55f409f48c7dfbaca38c76f27a0deabe7b16b97c2a789.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386846} {:answer/id #uuid "071a1b5d-cfe5-55c4-abfe-64aaa7d45f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3dc961eb8efb54df010577a633aea536f30d612a919cbde3cc9a18b82736ddd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffedee4a-7324-59fb-a847-2555b4c482e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386844} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4a0e42-49d6-587c-b083-d54401de0619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceebcce2-da76-556e-a74b-03eb33df1596" :question/math-academy-id "q-116942" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110569 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e88f43c-18c5-5fdb-b6d1-579370489099" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132a7d43-69b5-5d78-bd3f-e3ed18f336c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250718} {:answer/id #uuid "7953a171-a759-565d-85cf-6fd82a5fdec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251560} {:answer/id #uuid "90341be6-95a2-57a1-9256-6fd69e09cf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251777} {:answer/id #uuid "ac77906b-d8e7-58ad-9746-bb71682a0f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252055} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf5091f-632c-54c6-a3c8-b3a2b127d